{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And HaShem said to Moses.</b> It uses the language of soft speech, and also mentions the name of mercy — not on account of the one being sent against [Pharaoh], but on account of the emissary who is mentioned right next to it, ״to Moses,״ for these are matters in which a righteous person rejoices, as it says ״he will see vengeance״ (Psalms 58:11). Therefore it uses the language of saying and the mention of the name of mercy, for these are matters that sustain his heart." "<b>Further</b> — inasmuch as HaShem did not send the plague immediately and at once, but would first send to warn him beforehand — according to one view, one full week (Exodus Rabbah 9:12), and according to another, three weeks — and this is from the measure of kindness and mercy; therefore it uses the language of saying and the mention of the name of mercy." "It further intends, in the way I explained above in parashat Va'era (Exodus 6:2), that even the measure of mercy agreed with joy to execute judgment and to take vengeance against this enemy." "<b>For</b> I have hardened — meaning, from now on you will grasp and know that it is I who hardened his heart, for by a person's nature, even if his heart were hard as stone, he would tremble and quake upon seeing the plague of hail; yet this man remains in his wickedness — this is not within human nature, but rather Elohim decreed upon him to harden him. And according to what I explained in the verse (Exodus 9:32) ״and the wheat and the spelt, etc., for they are late,״ etc. — for HaShem performed wonders upon wonders, and this was a cause to harden his heart — it is understood as well, precisely, ״For I have hardened״ in performing this wonder." "<b>Or</b> it may intend this way: inasmuch as Moses saw the great plague of hail and this wicked man, on the contrary, only increased his sinning, he might lose hope and his heart might turn sour regarding his missions to Pharaoh. Therefore He said: do not be troubled by this, <b>for I have hardened his heart</b>, and were it not for this, when I release the hardness of heart from him, behold he sends them away." "<b>And et</b> the heart of his servants — the reason for the hardening of his servants' heart is so that they would not advise him to send them forth; also, through this the bringing of the plagues would be upon all of Egypt and not upon Pharaoh alone, for otherwise there would be no proclamation of the plagues that HaShem would bring upon Pharaoh alone. And although HaShem did not mention this detail in the first mission and said only ״I will harden the heart of Pharaoh,״ He stated the principal and included his servants within it. Or perhaps HaShem intended in His words here to explain to him His earlier words — that it was not Pharaoh's heart alone but also the heart of his servants. And the reason He did not explain it beforehand but explains it now is because Moses said in the previous parashah ״you and your servants״ (Exodus 9:30), revealing his opinion that he thinks the mention of his servants is not included in the mention of Pharaoh; and from now on he would understand from HaShem's words that He said to him ״I will harden the heart of Pharaoh״ — that his servants are not included — and he would think that the hardening of his servants' heart did not proceed from HaShem. Therefore He said <b>I have hardened his heart and the heart of his servants</b>." "<b>That I may set these My signs in his midst.</b> One needs to know why He was precise in saying <b>these My signs</b> — for it would appear that the principal reason He hardened his heart was for the sake of these signs, to place them, as though there is a need for the placing of these signs and no others. Further, the verse says <b>in his midst.</b> And this may be explained according to the way of their saying, of blessed memory (Exodus Rabbah 12:5), that the deeds of Egypt testify to the divinity — that He is the Sovereign and He is the Master — and the Master, because He desired Israel to bring them near to serve Him and to cleave to Him, desired to grant them insight to see and to behold with the eye, denying every faith except in Him. Yet He had not yet shown them that He creates wind and makes His angels winds, nor had He shown them that He creates light and darkness. Therefore He said <b>that I may set</b> etc. — meaning, because He said to him ״for I have hardened his heart,״ Moses has reason to say: why prolong the burden further and also need to ravage the orders of creation? For in every plague there is an alteration in the order of the arrangement of creation; if it is to show Him His might, may He be blessed, concerning which he said about Him ״Who is YHWH,״ did He not strike the plague of hail and show him HaShem's wondrous hand, and what need is there to overturn the order of creation again? Therefore He said to him the reason: <b>that I may set these My signs</b> — meaning, signs that I wish to perform in My world, so that Israel may recognize the divinity — these still remain for the completion of the faith. For in all seven plagues that have passed, there is in them no refutation of worshipping the sun or the moon; also for one who would claim that the Creator of wind is not the Creator of dust — although HaShem struck the earth, by what would it be known that He creates wind, that He forms man and shapes a form? And HaShem needed to show them through the deed of the locusts that He makes His angels winds, to bring them and also to return them, and also to carry up the dead so that the Egyptians would not benefit from them (Exodus Rabbah 13:7); and through the plague of darkness, that He forms the luminaries and rules over them, and also the creation of darkness, and decreed upon it to serve as darkness for Egypt (Exodus Rabbah 14:1); and through the plague of the firstborn it became known that He forms a form within a form and recognizes the firstborn's drop (Bava Metzia 61b); also in the midst of this plague HaShem commanded the commandment (Exodus 13:2) ״Sanctify to Me every firstborn,״ and this would serve as a remembrance that He formed him from the womb to be His servant. And therefore, when this wicked man was found, HaShem hardened his heart <b>that I may set these My signs</b> — that I need to make as a sign and wonder for the reason mentioned — they shall be <b>in his midst</b>, such that he too will find in them cups of reeling also on account of his wickedness; and without this reason HaShem would not have ravaged the orders of creation for his sake alone."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:26:41.501022925Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}